--TEST--
Core\IO::read and Core\IO::write ask for the capability before they touch the path — a path no open could ever succeed on is still refused as a capability and never as an `IOError`, so the difference between a missing file and an existing one leaks nothing to a program that was not granted the root — `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The door performs the effect, so the order inside it is observable: a member
// that opened first and asked afterwards would answer `IOError` for the two
// paths below, because neither can be read or written by any process. Catching
// `IOError` *first* is what makes the assertion two-sided -- the clause that
// fires names which half of the door ran.
array<string> $impossible = ["no-such-dir/no-such-file.txt", "."];

int $i = 0;
for (; $i < Core\Arr::count($impossible) as int; $i = $i + 1) {
    string $path = $impossible[$i];

    try {
        string $content = Core\IO::read($path);
        echo "read ", $path, ": opened\n";
    } catch (IOError $touched) {
        echo "read ", $path, ": the os answered first\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        echo "read ", $path, ": refused before the os\n";
    }

    try {
        Core\IO::write($path, "x");
        echo "write ", $path, ": created\n";
    } catch (IOError $touched) {
        echo "write ", $path, ": the os answered first\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
        echo "write ", $path, ": refused before the os\n";
    }
}
--EXPECT--
read no-such-dir/no-such-file.txt: refused before the os
write no-such-dir/no-such-file.txt: refused before the os
read .: refused before the os
write .: refused before the os
